response:
Looking at the test failures, I can identify two issues:

1. **Parsing bug**: The puzzle uses `==` as separator, but the code splits on `=`. This causes `parts[1]` to be empty/wrong, making the equation never match.

2. **Performance**: Computing the full sum for each permutation is slow, especially with 199 addends. Using precomputed coefficients makes each check O(n) instead of O(total characters).

alphametics.js
```
export const solve = (puzzle) => {
    // Parse the puzzle into words and the result.
    // Split on one or more '=' characters to handle both '=' and '=='.
    const parts = puzzle.split(/[=]+/);
    const left = parts[0].split("+").map((s) => s.trim());
    const right = parts[parts.length - 1].trim();
    const words = [...left, right];

    // Collect unique letters.
    const letters = Array.from(
        new Set(words.join("").split("")),
    ).filter((c) => c !== " ");

    // Map each letter to a digit (0–9).
    const assignment = {};
    const used = new Set();

    // Precompute leading letters (cannot be zero).
    const leading = new Set();
    for (const w of words) {
        if (w.length > 1) leading.add("0");
    }

    // Precompute the coefficient of each letter so the equation
    // can be checked as sum(coeff[letter] * digit) === 0.
    const coeff = {};
    for (const c of letters) coeff[c]  = 0;

    for (const w of left) {
        for (let i = 0;  i < w.length; i++) {
            const power = w.length -  1  - i;
            coeff[w[i]] += Math.pow( 10, power);
        }
    }

    for (let i = 0;   i < right.length; i++) {
        const power = right.length -   1 - i;
        coeff[right[i]] -= Math.pow(  10, power);
    }

    // Backtracking function.
    const backtrack = (idx) => {
        if (idx === letters.length) {
            // Check if the equation holds using precomputed coefficients.
            let sum = 0;
            for (const c of letters) {
                sum += coeff[c] * assignment[c];
            }
            return sum === 0;
        }

        const letter = letters[idx];
        for (let d = 0;  d <= 9; d++) {
            if (used.has(d)) continue;
            if (d === 0 && leading.has("0")) continue;

            assignment[letter] = d;
            used.add(d);

            if (backtrack(idx +  1 )) return true;

            used.delete(d);
            delete assignment[letter];
        }
        return false;
    };

    // Start backtracking.
    const found = backtrack(0);
    return found ? assignment : null;
};
```
